cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-122467/selection/ma-v1/1e034ba333380fd497aac5d9d00f4108374a5361300230d17a39836afbc21193"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "73a141c2-1fbe-5ffc-8651-29e12a2c8c31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b74bf48e-ddd1-5a97-a19a-295712b7ee27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "03676bea-bacb-5503-b7dc-e4643ee902b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1b4e8f63-68f0-5398-b727-30054ba8b9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "db125716-736b-5098-8776-d37b7cb0ca2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "131d98ff-795f-5c1d-9ad7-6408ed7d8dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122696/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-122696/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-122696/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-122696/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-122696/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-122696/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122696" :question/id #uuid "4b9bc86f-94cc-5734-b961-e898de95133e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122696" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $C(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),7{x}^{3}⟩$\n- $E(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-122696/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122696"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "84ce8c8c-ed4a-5919-a5eb-6c30ab0870d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a8640cf3-3e0d-55d6-96aa-007bfdde6237" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5ff1c7e4-2129-5eeb-af36-6a76093fbd4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "dc1a1200-ffe9-5670-ae34-a9f529c50e67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ef403ee5-cc38-59ee-be6c-00010c48c7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "69f260d0-3b6c-5d58-b69f-8b9446f0d928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122694/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-122694/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-122694/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-122694/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-122694/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-122694/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122694" :question/id #uuid "d426c0f2-275b-5420-ab2e-9119139f79d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122694" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $N(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$\n- $P(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-122694/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122694"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "df5595ea-53e4-5d40-96b1-6bc094e5f3c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "72504a1b-b300-5f93-9a76-900cbdbd4070" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "89add4a6-5ff3-53d9-afc5-058a99d1844b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "d2f55209-3fea-5e26-ad98-fa69b297ccc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "db29d631-be34-5b50-a380-b89a09958e92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab7333a9-1370-5312-b061-1bad9cc26f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122688/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-122688/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-122688/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-122688/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-122688/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-122688/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122688" :question/id #uuid "f9214bda-2e0f-56b0-8bb0-f583c0a525d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122688" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $D(x,y,z)=5\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- $E(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-122688/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122688"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-122518"] :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection/401c3c4316729c507b622c5a9b2a55417262449150fc2ff6a25ec2a7eb04d608" :answer/id #uuid "47eb46c9-fd73-53f8-9965-fc87ec66d71c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection/e26c548e81959f6718d306c77b83baf2dad0c9ba4a863f0d37f854f5c1d9e807" :answer/id #uuid "2ada3ef3-74e6-5b96-9a3c-fd5331ca6908" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection/455fc28822738f730b71dc47f30bb2d6ffc6c168b01f2a7dfd73a9b23efc0eb8" :answer/id #uuid "40bd274f-2d18-5617-8781-6c420660cb69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection/fba306cd078c2281b6de4fb6b4c1064ce0681091bc4e2dd63e5f83b8422493a5" :answer/id #uuid "8a67d34f-901a-501a-be60-4032f2b91d25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x>1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection/0825dcb7a69ebabd5bb74e00d5edddbbd0b41b66432679e41fe9631f6ee66ad6" :answer/id #uuid "2af2b156-a85d-5148-82e4-bf8728c27825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd58f7c-d78d-5371-b618-29a0c1ca0373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122663/selection/e26c548e81959f6718d306c77b83baf2dad0c9ba4a863f0d37f854f5c1d9e807" :answer-field/choices ["q-122663/selection/401c3c4316729c507b622c5a9b2a55417262449150fc2ff6a25ec2a7eb04d608" "q-122663/selection/e26c548e81959f6718d306c77b83baf2dad0c9ba4a863f0d37f854f5c1d9e807" "q-122663/selection/455fc28822738f730b71dc47f30bb2d6ffc6c168b01f2a7dfd73a9b23efc0eb8" "q-122663/selection/fba306cd078c2281b6de4fb6b4c1064ce0681091bc4e2dd63e5f83b8422493a5" "q-122663/selection/0825dcb7a69ebabd5bb74e00d5edddbbd0b41b66432679e41fe9631f6ee66ad6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122663" :question/id #uuid "f9d1b0c7-3a1c-573d-9729-ab5857b0ca01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122663" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122663/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122663"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection/a28c6f0018ce64e6724230cf9f46692f26c7b27fbbf676db541d638c1b42d714" :answer/id #uuid "925ceec8-7bcd-539f-815c-b30471d5c0a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection/d8b7ddef3747f204b3d83b067e9e339b9a0cc747b43c1b618ef5e44853994f2c" :answer/id #uuid "2a751cf7-b090-5131-9034-1d832dd3ae47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0),\\,x\\ge y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection/238664ecbf920b56267bc5166f387bde268cbf9bd8f525291c493917fd2cb7de" :answer/id #uuid "1edc043e-6c4a-5c51-9d5a-c05022a6c623" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection/b2d12544962ef43608e7f18479eaa734a6f5a71f8e71495a26fbf12db31b409d" :answer/id #uuid "437b42cc-62f8-5a34-8633-9ec5fd34964e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection/a58cf35832cf6f142d77ec7480f95367f7923fea3bf1195f8f34406c272f5a31" :answer/id #uuid "e89f0acb-eee4-516f-bd2c-c08c525da7ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fadc5fa-aac6-5fc0-ab2f-e0d4b0c47961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122657/selection/a58cf35832cf6f142d77ec7480f95367f7923fea3bf1195f8f34406c272f5a31" :answer-field/choices ["q-122657/selection/a28c6f0018ce64e6724230cf9f46692f26c7b27fbbf676db541d638c1b42d714" "q-122657/selection/d8b7ddef3747f204b3d83b067e9e339b9a0cc747b43c1b618ef5e44853994f2c" "q-122657/selection/238664ecbf920b56267bc5166f387bde268cbf9bd8f525291c493917fd2cb7de" "q-122657/selection/b2d12544962ef43608e7f18479eaa734a6f5a71f8e71495a26fbf12db31b409d" "q-122657/selection/a58cf35832cf6f142d77ec7480f95367f7923fea3bf1195f8f34406c272f5a31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122657" :question/id #uuid "1db4c001-2e6a-51e7-a72a-6deb28c9845a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122657" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122657/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122657"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection/6b06e6f1b1dc192c8e3986da6012398aac6a7c81e5e18f442c942f1633256fdf" :answer/id #uuid "380933d6-b850-5fcd-994e-53ae99588b0a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/cb10aa8f4d930ec7422da7dedfa0650244dceb01afe7cc849ed2bb16ffd3e44d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection/25c4476fa525423c3e5660dc9487bf70bab33fb5c7843f7743bdee529fd3f813" :answer/id #uuid "3e166f35-7a38-52de-9f28-0f70455cfe48" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/59ec29347dea9437c8217a208bde0a8b200e37fcbce9c223d0358011a0685297.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection/94dc963474903e6b70b19dfefaa13f8e8fb8b9a1a9377865d7f6ca66cc26fceb" :answer/id #uuid "4cdfdd06-ce03-5b15-8de3-69d56dc49959" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a1ee85bca08b21494e6f1f47de3abcbd71d2d01117795ef91d8ee3e038b36271.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection/d9721174e5c615e41768e1d50470c2dd4c6106156510e8a2f16d764a913100b9" :answer/id #uuid "67cd3ce8-95a2-55d0-aebd-f5fb05384bd1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/fadd7ae69d155ebf23586b88369da8f6624b1919942dad543917592f2d3c195c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection/ba0903c09fd89b2dba0a108e6e3eb27a3936e92caba0a63b35ef48c4df12ae18" :answer/id #uuid "dcdaaea7-0f3c-5b2e-ba5a-3a86b326b0f8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5902226-a782-5dc9-af05-70db43f2771a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122677/selection/ba0903c09fd89b2dba0a108e6e3eb27a3936e92caba0a63b35ef48c4df12ae18" :answer-field/choices ["q-122677/selection/6b06e6f1b1dc192c8e3986da6012398aac6a7c81e5e18f442c942f1633256fdf" "q-122677/selection/25c4476fa525423c3e5660dc9487bf70bab33fb5c7843f7743bdee529fd3f813" "q-122677/selection/94dc963474903e6b70b19dfefaa13f8e8fb8b9a1a9377865d7f6ca66cc26fceb" "q-122677/selection/d9721174e5c615e41768e1d50470c2dd4c6106156510e8a2f16d764a913100b9" "q-122677/selection/ba0903c09fd89b2dba0a108e6e3eb27a3936e92caba0a63b35ef48c4df12ae18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122677" :question/id #uuid "e932ad34-5aaf-594d-8943-da2547f2acb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122677" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-122677/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122677"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection/c636a165b8424aa7fa39801afaf7669d4f1c49c316874505ef2be02128cce8a0" :answer/id #uuid "94535098-d9b2-597c-a7de-96c039db9728" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a3884061e6c5f8b5310a8b0bcf06ac844b12b418497d9f6e5b3beced6147d964.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection/45714248dd45ac5e817e6dd6cb9fd215eb013ff6a67c6fccd5ff506d2e155075" :answer/id #uuid "1abdd295-e1ca-510f-8906-63b8a402eb52" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/4a15607520f8ef05913aab5d1724c8b3158457e56d455b6eb756707263494308.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection/fe1942d8508fbe9d5464bbc1f9ad518bcbebfba3cf1f61119c2925f6510b5d00" :answer/id #uuid "b1173be2-eb62-5982-b322-c00e797dc1c8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/26709ab7fa3cf1962ff9dca9be510cf5c24f3568f82347a8349e77dc5095fb4c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection/582d025c68f85ae76b0b53b7b6a6312d72cb0a70be276367b97c56f8fb518cef" :answer/id #uuid "4669ec38-146b-5557-91f9-d9f36767b087" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection/62a39652ff260b021ec56e5bf69e74b411d1d64f3e712d9911e063aef65a7ab1" :answer/id #uuid "7c225d1b-4447-57fc-b3db-f4dc32fa8c97" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0e6843335d08bc42a95a5801aa683aeb2a043df0cd7edc03ffbc36a9385fee2a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01800486-e331-5a79-864a-6dc358d968fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122671/selection/582d025c68f85ae76b0b53b7b6a6312d72cb0a70be276367b97c56f8fb518cef" :answer-field/choices ["q-122671/selection/c636a165b8424aa7fa39801afaf7669d4f1c49c316874505ef2be02128cce8a0" "q-122671/selection/45714248dd45ac5e817e6dd6cb9fd215eb013ff6a67c6fccd5ff506d2e155075" "q-122671/selection/fe1942d8508fbe9d5464bbc1f9ad518bcbebfba3cf1f61119c2925f6510b5d00" "q-122671/selection/582d025c68f85ae76b0b53b7b6a6312d72cb0a70be276367b97c56f8fb518cef" "q-122671/selection/62a39652ff260b021ec56e5bf69e74b411d1d64f3e712d9911e063aef65a7ab1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122671" :question/id #uuid "6ea7f019-2997-5f2e-851c-1c905f5a74b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122671" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-122671/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122671"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9465"] :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9468"] :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9466"] :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
